s consideration. Trusted Execution Environmen</description></item><item><title>RegionAlign: Manipulating Parameter Layouts for Localized Fault Injection on LLMs and VLMs</title><link>https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.33370</link><guid isPermaLink="false">b7b70b0f9928</guid><pubDate>Thu, 26 Nov 2026 05:00:00 -0000</pubDate><category>untagged</category><description>arXiv cs.CR: arXiv:2611.33370v1 Announce Type: new Abstract: Physical fault injection can flip stored weight bits, degrading LLMs and VLMs. Existing work largely overlooks how supply-chain manipulation affects fault vulnerability. We introduce RegionAlign, which allows a malicious provider to manipulate only the parameter layout before deployment, using bit-sensitivity search and channel permutations to concen</description></item><item><title>Poisoning Self-Evolving Security Policies in LLM Agents</title><link>https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.33385</link><guid isPermaLink="false">ed35a45a710c</guid><pubDate>Thu, 26 Nov 2026 05:00:00 -0000</pubDate><category>untagged</category><description>arXiv cs.CR: arXiv:2611.33385v1 Announce Type: new Abstract: Self-evolving defenses improve LLM-agent security by learning reusable policies from observed attacks. However, we demonstrate that this learning process itself can become an attack surface. Using SED (Self-Evolving Defense) in the AgentDojo benchmark, we construct a single prompt-injection episode that causes the defense to learn an over-broad polic</description></item><item><title>SecID: An LLM-Guided Framework for Threat-Aware Secondary Asset Identification with Quantitative RTL Analysis</title><link>https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.33453</link><guid isPermaLink="false">427d8bb5d081</guid><pubDate>Thu, 26 Nov 2026 05:00:00 -0000</pubDate><category>untagged</category><description>arXiv cs.CR: arXiv:2611.33453v1 Announce Type: new Abstract: The growing complexity of modern hardware designs is making security assurance increasingly difficult. One of the foundational steps in pre-si